WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:42.570 --> 00:00:45.420
Burkina Faso is one of
many developing nations

00:00:45.420 --> 00:00:47.550
swapping traditional earth brick buildings

00:00:47.550 --> 00:00:50.280
for those made of concrete and cement.

00:00:50.280 --> 00:00:51.690
While these building materials

00:00:51.690 --> 00:00:53.970
are often seen as a sign of development,

00:00:53.970 --> 00:00:56.580
the reality is quite different.

00:00:56.580 --> 00:00:58.380
The construction industry globally

00:00:58.380 --> 00:01:01.920
accounts for 38% of
carbon dioxide emissions

00:01:01.920 --> 00:01:06.090
and building with concrete makes
up around 8% of that total.

00:01:06.090 --> 00:01:09.030
We architects have to start listening

00:01:09.030 --> 00:01:12.600
not just to the climate and to ecology.

00:01:12.600 --> 00:01:15.900
We seriously have to kind of own up

00:01:15.900 --> 00:01:18.420
to the responsibility
that's at stake here.

00:01:18.420 --> 00:01:21.990
I think this is the responsibility
of every future architect

00:01:21.990 --> 00:01:25.140
to think about mitigating the damage

00:01:25.140 --> 00:01:27.753
and to look at the proper alternatives.

00:01:29.880 --> 00:01:32.340
Clara Sawadogo is one of those architects

00:01:32.340 --> 00:01:34.473
looking for a sustainable alternative.

00:01:35.340 --> 00:01:37.140
She's part of a growing movement

00:01:37.140 --> 00:01:39.570
who think reimagining
how we build with mud

00:01:39.570 --> 00:01:40.833
is the solution.

00:02:05.010 --> 00:02:07.110
Sawadogo often uses banco,

00:02:07.110 --> 00:02:09.453
also known as adobe, in her buildings.

00:02:10.350 --> 00:02:12.150
The compacted sun-dried bricks

00:02:12.150 --> 00:02:14.880
make use of whatever can
be found in the local area,

00:02:14.880 --> 00:02:18.543
including soil, sand,
clay, stones, and sticks.

00:02:58.920 --> 00:03:00.960
Mud buildings gained a bad reputation

00:03:00.960 --> 00:03:02.160
in Burkina Faso

00:03:02.160 --> 00:03:04.740
after there were several
reports of houses collapsing

00:03:04.740 --> 00:03:06.243
due to extreme rainfall.

00:03:07.200 --> 00:03:08.910
As living standards have increased

00:03:08.910 --> 00:03:11.610
and cement has become
more easily accessible,

00:03:11.610 --> 00:03:14.193
people have moved away
from using earth to build.

00:03:38.701 --> 00:03:40.020
Sawadogo was convinced

00:03:40.020 --> 00:03:42.840
that mud can not only withstand
changes to the environment

00:03:42.840 --> 00:03:44.943
but actually far better than concrete.

00:03:45.840 --> 00:03:47.910
Her buildings also challenge the idea

00:03:47.910 --> 00:03:49.623
of what mud buildings look like.

00:04:18.420 --> 00:04:20.880
The Nubian vault is a
design that was revived

00:04:20.880 --> 00:04:24.570
by Egyptian architect
Hassan Fathy in the 1940s

00:04:24.570 --> 00:04:27.090
after he saw the technique
in the Nubian village

00:04:27.090 --> 00:04:28.950
of Abu al-Riche.

00:04:28.950 --> 00:04:30.360
It has a curved structure

00:04:30.360 --> 00:04:33.150
that doesn't require wood
or metal reinforcement,

00:04:33.150 --> 00:04:35.010
making it environmentally friendly

00:04:35.010 --> 00:04:38.277
and ideal for countries with
limited natural resources.

00:05:15.780 --> 00:05:17.370
Beyond being a recyclable

00:05:17.370 --> 00:05:19.050
and sustainable resource,

00:05:19.050 --> 00:05:22.500
earth is also better suited
to rising temperatures.

00:05:22.500 --> 00:05:24.240
Mud has a high thermal mass,

00:05:24.240 --> 00:05:26.610
which means it slowly
absorbs and stores heat

00:05:26.610 --> 00:05:27.450
during the day,

00:05:27.450 --> 00:05:29.673
stopping a house from becoming too hot.

00:05:30.660 --> 00:05:32.550
Then, when the temperature drops at night,

00:05:32.550 --> 00:05:34.890
the heat is radiated back into the room.

00:06:03.870 --> 00:06:04.980
In contrast,

00:06:04.980 --> 00:06:07.650
thin cinder block walls
with hollow recesses

00:06:07.650 --> 00:06:09.990
allow heat to pass through much quicker,

00:06:09.990 --> 00:06:12.900
causing the inside of
buildings to get extremely hot

00:06:12.900 --> 00:06:14.370
during the day.

00:06:14.370 --> 00:06:16.350
Many people who've built with concrete

00:06:16.350 --> 00:06:19.380
are finding they now need to
buy air conditioning units

00:06:19.380 --> 00:06:21.570
but the cost of installation and upkeep

00:06:21.570 --> 00:06:23.670
are too much for some.

00:06:23.670 --> 00:06:25.740
Our institutions at the moment

00:06:25.740 --> 00:06:29.910
are geared towards this industrial form

00:06:29.910 --> 00:06:34.170
of computer type of generated architecture

00:06:34.170 --> 00:06:37.320
which is devoid of any sensitivity,

00:06:37.320 --> 00:06:40.320
devoid of any interaction with nature,

00:06:40.320 --> 00:06:43.080
devoid of any interaction
with the inhabitants

00:06:43.080 --> 00:06:44.520
who are going to be living there,

00:06:44.520 --> 00:06:48.090
let alone the discussion of a community.

00:06:48.090 --> 00:06:50.130
As Hassan Fathy used to say,

00:06:50.130 --> 00:06:52.110
we're interested in the people

00:06:52.110 --> 00:06:54.300
who have the 'malaliem,'

00:06:54.300 --> 00:06:55.470
meaning the cents,

00:06:55.470 --> 00:06:56.820
and not in the people

00:06:56.820 --> 00:06:57.960
who have the 'malayien,'

00:06:57.960 --> 00:06:59.940
meaning the millions, you know.

00:06:59.940 --> 00:07:01.980
We're talking of the wider population

00:07:01.980 --> 00:07:05.820
because this is where
the impact of the climate

00:07:05.820 --> 00:07:09.603
and what's happening in the
climate change is most seen.

00:07:10.890 --> 00:07:12.360
Burkina Faso contributes

00:07:12.360 --> 00:07:15.540
just 0.08% of global emissions,

00:07:15.540 --> 00:07:18.000
but ranks as one of the
country's most vulnerable

00:07:18.000 --> 00:07:19.083
to climate change.

00:07:20.070 --> 00:07:22.650
The landlocked nation could
see a temperature increase

00:07:22.650 --> 00:07:26.130
of up to 1.6 degrees Celsius by 2050,

00:07:26.130 --> 00:07:29.340
and up to four degrees by
the end of the century.

00:07:29.340 --> 00:07:32.313
That's two and a half times
more than the global average.

00:07:33.570 --> 00:07:36.750
We need a paradigm shift,

00:07:36.750 --> 00:07:40.860
and unless this happens
yesterday before today,

00:07:40.860 --> 00:07:43.020
it's not going to be possible.

00:07:43.020 --> 00:07:44.910
It's not going to be possible

00:07:44.910 --> 00:07:49.800
to mitigate the effects
of the carbon footprint

00:07:49.800 --> 00:07:54.480
and the damage, the damage
that we have been creating.

00:07:54.480 --> 00:07:55.650
There's this balance,

00:07:55.650 --> 00:07:57.690
there's this ecological balance

00:07:57.690 --> 00:08:00.540
about the use of natural
building materials

00:08:00.540 --> 00:08:03.300
that you don't find anymore.

00:08:03.300 --> 00:08:05.400
This is not the architecture of the past.

00:08:05.400 --> 00:08:07.473
This is the architecture of the future.

00:08:08.670 --> 00:08:10.890
Building with mud offers clear advantages

00:08:10.890 --> 00:08:12.030
for the environment,

00:08:12.030 --> 00:08:14.640
especially for those
living in hotter climates,

00:08:14.640 --> 00:08:17.160
but it's not without its drawbacks.

00:08:17.160 --> 00:08:19.290
Creating structures like Sawadogo's

00:08:19.290 --> 00:08:22.020
that are as sturdy as concrete
and look just as modern

00:08:22.020 --> 00:08:23.910
takes much more time.

00:08:23.910 --> 00:08:25.620
This makes it difficult to scale up

00:08:25.620 --> 00:08:28.263
to match the demands of
the modern building sector.

00:08:29.490 --> 00:08:32.340
For now, architects are
using a hybrid method,

00:08:32.340 --> 00:08:35.370
combining a small amount of
concrete into their designs,

00:08:35.370 --> 00:08:36.960
which speeds up the process

00:08:36.960 --> 00:08:40.157
but also safeguards against
more intense weather events.

